<title>USA: U.S. Jan construction spending rose 0.4 pct.</title>
<headline>U.S. Jan construction spending rose 0.4 pct.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-03-03</dateline>
<text>
<p>U.S. Commerce Department seasonally adjusted construction spending, with comparisons.</p>
<p> PERCENT CHANGE:     Jan     Dec--(Prev)   Nov-(Prev) Jan97/96</p>
<p> Total Spending	0.4    -0.9   -0.7    2.2    2.0     5.5</p>
<p> ANNUAL RATES, BILLIONS OF U.S. DOLLARS:		    Jan'96</p>
<p> Total Spending    589.8   587.4  587.7  592.6  591.6   559.0</p>
<p> Private Spending  445.7   443.4  441.5  444.7  443.5   418.9</p>
<p> -Residential	249.7   248.6  248.0  248.1  247.0   242.5</p>
<p> --New Housing     176.6   176.3  176.4  177.2  177.2   169.2</p>
<p> ---Single-Family  156.6   156.6  156.0  156.5  156.5   149.8</p>
<p> ---Multi-Family    20.0    19.7   20.4   20.7   20.7    19.4</p>
<p>ANNUAL RATES, BILLIONS OF U.S. DOLLARS:</p>
<p> -			 Jan     Dec--(Prev)   Nov-(Prev)  Jan'96</p>
<p> -Nonresidential   153.8   149.5  151.2  154.2  154.1  137.4</p>
<p> --Industrial	 30.0    29.4   28.9   31.6   31.6   32.5</p>
<p> --Office	     27.9    27.6   27.6   28.7   28.7   22.3</p>
<p> Public Spending   144.1   144.0  146.1  147.9  148.0  140.1</p>
<p> PERCENT CHANGE:     Jan     Dec--(Prev)   Nov-(Prev) Jan97/96</p>
<p> Private Spending    0.5    -0.3   -0.5    2.5    2.2    6.4</p>
<p> Public Spending    Unch    -2.6   -1.3    1.2    1.3    2.9</p>
<p>Construction spending in 1992 dollars, which discounts the effects of inflation:</p>
<p> PERCENT CHANGE:     Jan     Dec--(Prev)   Nov--(Prev)</p>
<p>  Total Spending     0.5    -1.1   -1.0    1.7    1.8</p>
<p>ANNUAL RATES, BILLIONS OF U.S. DOLLARS:</p>
<p>  Total Spending   506.7   504.1  505.9  509.9  510.8</p>
<p>The January level of $445.7 billion in private construction starts was the highest on record, the department said.</p>
</text>
